::CW_U05_PRE_Q01::Draft problem: A peer says the ending is unclear because the reader cannot tell why the character leaves. What is the best next step?{
~A. Change every character name.#Incorrect. Names do not address motivation.
=B. Add or revise a moment that shows the character's reason for leaving.#Correct. This responds directly to the feedback.
~C. Ignore the feedback because all endings are confusing.#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered.
~D. Add random punctuation.#Incorrect. Punctuation will not clarify motivation.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q02::Workshop context: Which feedback is most useful?{
~A. I do not like stories like this.#Incorrect. This is personal preference.
~B. It is good.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~C. Change everything.#Incorrect. This is too broad.
=D. The repeated hallway image builds tension; consider clarifying what the character fears at the end.#Correct. This names a strength and a focused suggestion.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q03::Format context: A script has dialogue but no character labels. What should be revised?{
=A. Add clear speaker labels and useful stage directions.#Correct. This supports script readability.
~B. Remove all dialogue.#Incorrect. Dialogue is central to script form.
~C. Add random stanza breaks.#Incorrect. Stanza breaks do not solve script speaker clarity.
~D. Ignore formatting.#Incorrect. Format matters.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q04::Which answer best explains author craft reasoning?{
~A. I like my poem.#Incorrect. This is opinion.
~B. The poem has words.#Incorrect. This is too general.
=C. I placed the final image on its own line so the reader pauses before the speaker's decision.#Correct. This names a craft choice and reader effect.
~D. I wrote it quickly.#Incorrect. Speed is not craft reasoning.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q05::Which piece is most publication-ready?{
=A. A revised poem with purposeful line breaks, checked conventions, clear title, and appropriate content.#Correct. This includes revision, format, conventions, and audience readiness.
~B. A rough draft with no title.#Incorrect. It lacks readiness checks.
~C. A script with no speaker labels.#Incorrect. This has a format issue.
~D. A story with random spacing and no review.#Incorrect. Random spacing and no review weaken readiness.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q06::Feedback says a paragraph is confusing because two speakers are in one paragraph. Which revision best fits?{
~A. Add more unrelated setting.#Incorrect. The issue is speaker clarity.
~B. Change the title only.#Incorrect. The title does not solve dialogue confusion.
~C. Delete all dialogue.#Incorrect. A targeted revision is better.
=D. Start a new paragraph when the speaker changes and check dialogue tags.#Correct. This improves clarity and format.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q07::Which answer should be eliminated first?{
~A. Consider feedback and protect the writer's purpose.#Incorrect. This is strong.
=B. Accept every suggestion automatically.#Correct. Automatic acceptance can damage purpose and voice.
~C. Explain reader effect.#Incorrect. Reader effect is important.
~D. Check format before publication.#Incorrect. Format checks are important.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q08::A portfolio note should include what?{
~A. Only "I like it."#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. Private personal information.#Incorrect. That is inappropriate.
=C. Selection reason, revision evidence, and final readiness explanation.#Correct. This supports portfolio readiness.
~D. A list of random colors.#Incorrect. That does not explain writing growth.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q09::Which is the best author presentation opening?{
~A. My story has stuff in it.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. I will tell the whole plot first.#Incorrect. This over-summarizes.
~C. I do not know why I wrote it.#Incorrect. This lacks purpose.
=D. "Open Door" is a short story meant to show a character deciding whether to trust someone again.#Correct. This names title, form, and purpose.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q10::Which revision most directly improves reader effect?{
=A. Replacing "I was sad" with an image of the last bus passing without stopping.#Correct. This shows emotion through image.
~B. Changing the font randomly.#Incorrect. This does not improve the writing itself.
~C. Deleting the title for no reason.#Incorrect. That may weaken reader preparation.
~D. Ignoring feedback about clarity.#Incorrect. That does not improve reader effect.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q11::What is active listening in workshop?{
~A. Interrupting before the writer finishes.#Incorrect. That blocks listening.
=B. Restating the writer's purpose before giving feedback.#Correct. This shows understanding before response.
~C. Giving only personal preference.#Incorrect. Feedback should be craft-focused.
~D. Ignoring the writer's goal.#Incorrect. The goal matters.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q12::Which is a strong publication decision?{
~A. Post another student's work without permission.#Incorrect. That violates authorship.
=B. Use a school-appropriate excerpt and remove private identifying details.#Correct. This supports safe online publication.
~C. Publish a rough draft without review.#Incorrect. Readiness checks are needed.
~D. Mock peer writing in a comment.#Incorrect. Publication must be respectful.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q13::Which statement best separates revision and polishing?{
=A. Revision improves meaning or structure; polishing checks final details.#Correct. This separates major improvement from final cleanup.
~B. Polishing fixes purpose.#Incorrect. Purpose is a revision issue.
~C. Revision means random changes.#Incorrect. Revision is purposeful.
~D. Polishing should happen before reading the draft.#Incorrect. Polishing comes after review.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q14::A poem uses random line breaks that make the central image hard to read. What should the writer do?{
~A. Keep the random breaks because all poems must be confusing.#Incorrect. Format should support meaning.
~B. Delete every image.#Incorrect. The issue is line breaks.
~C. Ignore the reader.#Incorrect. Reader understanding matters.
=D. Revise line breaks so the image and pauses support the intended effect.#Correct. This improves format-specific quality.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q15::Which response best explains a revision decision?{
~A. I changed it because I had to.#Incorrect. This does not explain improvement.
~B. I changed random words.#Incorrect. Random change is not revision reasoning.
=C. I shortened the ending because feedback showed the first version explained too much.#Correct. This connects feedback, change, and improvement.
~D. I did not revise anything.#Incorrect. This does not explain revision.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q16::Which feedback is least helpful?{
~A. The opening image creates suspense.#Incorrect. This names a strength.
=B. Make it better.#Correct. This is too vague to guide revision.
~C. What does the character want in the final scene?#Incorrect. This is a useful question.
~D. Consider adding one sound before the reveal.#Incorrect. This is a focused suggestion.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q17::Which checklist best fits Unit 5?{
~A. Type, post, ignore feedback.#Incorrect. This skips the workflow.
~B. Decorate randomly and submit.#Incorrect. Random decoration is not readiness.
~C. Summarize the plot only.#Incorrect. Unit 5 includes more than summary.
=D. Understand purpose, consider feedback, revise, check format and conventions, prepare readers, explain author reasoning.#Correct. This synthesizes the unit.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q18::A writer's presentation retells every event but gives no craft reasoning. What is the best correction?{
=A. Focus on purpose, two craft choices, and one revision decision.#Correct. This matches author presentation expectations.
~B. Add more plot summary.#Incorrect. Summary is already the issue.
~C. Remove the creative work entirely.#Incorrect. The work should remain central.
~D. Avoid mentioning craft.#Incorrect. Craft reasoning is required.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q19::Which answer best handles online collaborative feedback?{
~A. Let reviewers erase the writer's purpose.#Incorrect. Purpose should be protected.
=B. Consider suggestions, accept what improves the piece, and reject what conflicts with purpose.#Correct. This is thoughtful collaboration.
~C. Reject every comment automatically.#Incorrect. Some feedback may help.
~D. Publish without review.#Incorrect. Review supports readiness.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q20::Which convention issue should be fixed before publication?{
=A. Dialogue punctuation that makes speakers hard to follow.#Correct. This affects clarity.
~B. A purposeful line break in a poem.#Incorrect. Purposeful line breaks may be effective.
~C. A strong title.#Incorrect. A strong title should remain.
~D. A clear author note.#Incorrect. This supports publication.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q21::Which selection rationale is strongest?{
=A. I selected this story because revision from feedback clarified the character's decision and made the ending stronger.#Correct. This connects selection to growth.
~B. I selected this because it was first in my folder.#Incorrect. That is not a quality reason.
~C. I selected this because I did not read the others.#Incorrect. That is not purposeful.
~D. I selected this because it has no title.#Incorrect. Missing title is not a strength.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q22::Which answer best identifies reader effect?{
~A. The writer changed something.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=B. The shorter sentence slows the reader at the final decision.#Correct. This explains effect.
~C. The piece exists.#Incorrect. This does not identify effect.
~D. The author used a computer.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q23::Which is the strongest response to vague feedback?{
=A. Ask for the specific place where the reader became confused.#Correct. This turns vague feedback into useful information.
~B. Accept it without question.#Incorrect. Vague feedback needs clarification.
~C. Delete the draft.#Incorrect. That is extreme.
~D. Ignore all future feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback can help.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q24::Which author note is best for publication?{
~A. This piece is about everything and nothing.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. This poem uses the bus stop image to show waiting; I revised the final line so the speaker's choice is clearer.#Correct. This gives context and revision insight.
~C. Do not read this.#Incorrect. This does not prepare readers.
~D. I will explain every word.#Incorrect. This overexplains.
}

::CW_U05_PRE_Q25::Which response shows Unit 5 readiness?{
=A. I can use feedback to revise, check format and conventions, prepare work for publication, and explain craft choices to an audience.#Correct. This summarizes the unit's major skills.
~B. I can type a rough draft and stop.#Incorrect. This skips the unit skills.
~C. I can ignore readers and feedback.#Incorrect. Reader effect and feedback matter.
~D. I can choose answers randomly.#Incorrect. Readiness requires purposeful reasoning.
}
